TITLE: DG-1A-AR-H143 — Adaptive Topology Repair / Split-Merge-Quarantine Recovery Audit Preregistration
DATE: 2026-09-19
STATUS: PREREGISTERED / SYNTHETIC / UNEXECUTED
TRACK: DG-1A / ADAPTIVE-RULE RESEARCH
BRANCH: dg1a-ar
PARENT: dg1a-arh142-proof-bearing-topology-audit.ice

PURPOSE

H142 established that:

calibrated topology confidence
+
high-confidence action cores
+
quarantine

can reduce cross-boundary false promotion.

But quarantine creates a new developmental failure mode:

UNCERTAIN MEMBERS
CAN BECOME
PERMANENTLY EXCLUDED.

That is safe in the short term.

It is not:
developmentally complete.

H143 asks:

CAN
REPEATED LOCAL EVIDENCE
REPAIR
THE TOPOLOGY
THAT CAUSED
THE QUARANTINE?

The target is not:
perfect clustering.

The target is:

BOUNDED
STRUCTURAL SELF-REPAIR

without:
oscillation,
over-fragmentation,
unsafe merge,
or:
unbounded always-active topology machinery.

BOUNDARY

Synthetic research only.

It does not:
- implement living or biological tissue;
- claim biological developmental equivalence;
- establish production clustering rules;
- execute or modify STAB-18-R1;
- spend canonical scientific execution;
- authorize runtime activation.

EVIDENCE CLASS TARGET

SYNTHETIC
MEASURED_SANDBOX
LONGITUDINAL

LONGITUDINAL ECOLOGY

Family population:

60 to 100 members.

Initial latent true subfamilies:

4 to 6.

Episode horizon:

80 episodes.

At each episode:

- 20% to 50% of members become active;
- a bounded pairwise/local relational evidence budget is available;
- dormant members remain represented but do not require continuous topology computation.

Relational evidence asks:

DO THESE TWO MEMBERS
CURRENTLY BEHAVE
AS THE SAME
FUNCTIONAL SUBFAMILY?

Primary evidence noise:

5%.

Evidence is local and pairwise.

No policy receives:
the latent true partition.

TOPOLOGY EVENTS

E0 — STABLE

True partition remains unchanged.

E1 — SPLIT

At a preregistered episode,
one true subfamily separates into:
two coherent descendants.

E2 — MERGE

Two true subfamilies become:
functionally exchangeable
and should become one group.

E3 — BOUNDARY DRIFT

A minority of members gradually migrate
from one functional subfamily to another.

E4 — TOPOLOGY DAMAGE

A fraction of stored observed labels / confidence state is:
erased,
corrupted,
or randomized.

Primary damage fractions:

10%,
25%,
40%.

E5 — TRANSIENT FALSE SHIFT

For a short interval,
noisy evidence mimics a real split or merge,
then disappears.

This tests:
structural overreaction.

REPRESENTATION

The organism stores:

CURRENT PARTITION

plus optionally:

CANDIDATE PARTITION

and:

LOCAL RELATIONAL EVIDENCE DEBT.

A candidate topology is not immediately accepted.

It may:
accumulate evidence,
hibernate,
or:
be discarded.

POLICIES

A — STATIC QUARANTINE

H142-style topology.

No topology mutation.

Boundary uncertainty only:
shrinks action core
and:
quarantines members.

B — GREEDY REPAIR

Immediately reassign,
split,
or merge
when current evidence crosses a threshold.

No hysteresis.

This is the overreaction comparator.

C — HYSTERETIC REPAIR

Topology changes require:

- evidence margin;
- repeated confirmation across multiple episodes;
- cooldown after accepted structural change.

Candidate topology remains separate from:
accepted topology

until confirmation.

D — DUAL-PARTITION HIBERNATION

Maintain:

accepted partition
and:
one bounded alternate candidate.

The candidate may hibernate with:
low active cost.

When fresh evidence returns:

reactivate the candidate
and compare:

old topology
versus:
candidate topology.

No unbounded candidate population.

E — REGENERATIVE REPAIR

Uses HYSTERETIC / DUAL logic
plus:

after topology damage,
reconstruct uncertain boundaries from:
surviving local relational evidence.

No privileged original labels.

F — ORACLE CURRENT PARTITION

Synthetic reference only.

Uses latent current partition.

Not a deployable policy.

PAIRWISE EVIDENCE BUDGET

Primary:

up to 2N pair observations per episode.

Robustness:

0.5N,
N,
2N,
4N.

Evidence sampling favors:

- active members;
- quarantined members;
- high-uncertainty boundaries;
- recently contradictory members.

A uniform-sampling comparator is also recorded.

REPAIR OPERATIONS

REASSIGN

Move one member
from current subfamily
to another candidate subfamily.

SPLIT

Create:
one new subfamily

from:
a coherent dissenting subset.

Minimum split support:

at least 5 members
and:
persistent evidence.

MERGE

Combine two subfamilies only if:

cross-group same-family evidence
remains high
across repeated episodes.

HIBERNATE

Candidate topology consumes:
bounded metadata

but:
no continuous full-family compute.

REJECT

Discard a candidate
when evidence decays or reverses.

HYSTERESIS

Primary working rule:

structural candidate must remain preferred
for at least:
3 consecutive evidence checkpoints

before acceptance.

Cooldown:

5 episodes
after accepted:
split,
merge,
or:
large reassignment.

Exact thresholds are synthetic working values.

They are frozen before execution.

PRIMARY METRICS

1. pairwise partition accuracy;
2. adjusted Rand / equivalent partition agreement;
3. quarantined member fraction;
4. quarantined active-value fraction;
5. structural mutation count;
6. false split count;
7. false merge count;
8. topology churn;
9. recovery episodes after real split;
10. recovery episodes after real merge;
11. recovery episodes after topology damage;
12. action-core coverage;
13. cross-boundary false promotion proxy;
14. evidence observations/episode;
15. active topology-maintenance cost;
16. dormant candidate storage cost.

PRIMARY ACCEPTANCE SHAPE

Adaptive topology repair is supported if:

- HYSTERETIC or DUAL/REGENERATIVE repair materially reduces long-run quarantine relative to STATIC;
- real split and merge events are recovered within bounded episodes;
- recovery uses bounded local evidence rather than full all-pairs observation;
- false structural mutations are materially lower than GREEDY under transient false shifts;
- topology damage can be partially regenerated without restoring privileged original labels;
- accepted topology does not oscillate indefinitely;
- candidate hibernation reduces active topology cost relative to recomputing all alternatives every episode;
- improved topology translates into lower cross-boundary false-promotion proxy or larger safe action cores;
- the policy does not explode the number of subfamilies.

FAILURE CONDITIONS

Treat H143 as mixed/negative if:

- repair only works with near all-pairs evidence;
- hysteresis prevents timely real adaptation;
- greedy and hysteretic policies are indistinguishable;
- split/merge oscillation persists;
- false shifts cause lasting fragmentation;
- damage recovery simply memorizes original labels;
- candidate hibernation accumulates unbounded dormant alternatives;
- partition accuracy improves without reducing quarantine or action error;
- repair cost becomes continuously active and scales approximately quadratically with family size.

PRIMARY EVENT SCHEDULE

Episodes 1..20:
stable baseline.

Episode 21:
real split event.

Episodes 21..35:
post-split adaptation window.

Episode 36:
25% topology-damage event.

Episodes 36..50:
regeneration window.

Episode 51:
real merge event.

Episodes 51..65:
post-merge adaptation window.

Episodes 66..70:
transient false-shift stress.

Episodes 71..80:
return to stable ecology.

Separate one-event controls will isolate:
split,
merge,
damage,
and:
false shift.

OUT-OF-BOX EXTENSION

The main architectural idea is:

TOPOLOGY
SHOULD HAVE
A DEVELOPMENTAL LIFECYCLE.

It can:

form,
mature,
be challenged,
split,
merge,
hibernate,
be damaged,
and:
regenerate.

But:

STRUCTURAL CHANGE
SHOULD ITSELF
BE EVIDENCE-BOUND.

This creates a possible hierarchy:

MEMBER EVIDENCE
->
BOUNDARY EVIDENCE
->
TOPOLOGY CANDIDATE
->
ACCEPTED TOPOLOGY
->
ACTION SCOPE.

No layer gains authority
merely because:
it is newer
or:
more active.

